8.1.0 (2026-08-22)

    GUI:
	- New feature: The Burba instrument sensible heat correction is greyed out, and
	               its setting cleared, on a project whose metadata describes no
	               LI-7500 family analyser.
	               The engine already refuses it in that case - OverrideSettings walks
	               every gas column and forces bu_corr to 'none' if none names a model
	               containing li7500 - but it does so SILENTLY, with no warning number,
	               unlike the conditional eddy covariance partition which at least says
	               Warning(114). So the box could be ticked, saved, and run, and the
	               correction quietly not happen. Now the interface and the run agree.
	               The model set is exactly the engine's four open-path variants -
	               LI-7500, LI-7500A, LI-7500RS, LI-7500DS. LI-7200 and LI-7700 do not
	               qualify, which is what the engine's substring test already implies.
	               This is the only control on the Advanced pages that depends on the
	               METADATA rather than on the project, so it listens to DlProject:
	               adding an analyser in the Metadata File Editor enables the box
	               without reopening the project.
	               No SmartFlux veto, unlike the partition. The engine clears bu_corr in
	               exactly three places - express mode, metadata retrieval, and the
	               LI-7500 test itself - and none of them is the embedded run
	               environment; SmartFlux is also the case where the correction matters
	               most, being paired with the open-path head it describes.
	               The list walk behind it now lives in IrgaDesc, because the spectral
	               page asks the same question for its Massman fallback and the two had
	               a copy each: they agreed on which models count and could still have
	               disagreed on the answer.
	- New feature: Advanced Settings > Processing Options > Other options gains
	               "Parallelise the planar fit and time lag pre-passes", which passes
	               the engine's new -j switch when a run is started.
	               Before it computes any flux the engine may walk every averaging
	               period once, to fit the planar fit planes or to optimise the time
	               lags. Each period in that walk is independent of the others, so it
	               is split across the processor cores; the results are identical to a
	               serial run.
	               The state lives in the application preferences, not in the project.
	               How many cores this computer should give the engine has nothing to
	               do with the site being processed, so there is no new ini key, the
	               generated tag tables do not move, and ecproject.cpp is untouched.
	               -j is passed in BOTH states, "0" for auto-detect and "1" for serial:
	               the engine's own default with no -j is to use every core, so an
	               unticked box that passed nothing would do nothing.
	               Off by default until the serial-versus-parallel equivalence runs are
	               signed off; only the two eddyflow_rp launches get it, since FCC has
	               no pre-pass and metadata retrieval never reaches one.
	- Fix: The day and night tabs of the Burba multiple-regression parameters
       could not be clicked. The Conditional Eddy Covariance checkbox was
       added to the grid at row 27, which the Burba parameter stack already
       spans: two widgets in one cell overlap, and the one added later is
       painted over what is underneath it - here, the tab bar. The checkbox
       now sits on row 33, beside its own settings button, the way every
       other option carrying one does.
       The layout stretch moves off row 27 for the same reason. Giving it to
       a row that holds a widget lets that block absorb the slack instead of
       the empty space below the group.
	- Fix: An imported EddyUH project could not be run. It carried no gas records,
       no master sonic, no column selection and no external-metadata flag, so
       the engine found no gas analyser, reported an acquisition frequency of
       zero, parsed no record from any raw file, and ended with "EddyFlow was
       not able to process any raw file" - which names none of that. Five
       things were missing, each found by running the imported project:
         * use_pfile. An EddyUH project always describes generic ASCII files,
           which carry no embedded metadata, so the pair's metadata file is
           the only description of the columns there is. Without the flag the
           engine never opens it.
         * The gas and cell records. The metadata says what a column IS; the
           project says which columns the run should USE. In the interface
           these are filled by the metadata read that follows an import; they
           are now built by the importer as well, so a project converted
           without a window open is runnable.
         * master_sonic. One sonic, nothing to choose between - but left
           empty the engine has no wind at all.
         * col_ts, col_air_t and col_air_p. Their state default is -1,
           "nothing decided", which the engine reads as no selection at all.
           Zero is "nothing selected here", which is what a project that
           describes its columns through the records means.
         * pr_start_time and pr_end_time. The engine honours a date range
           only when the subset flag is on AND both times are set; with
           either empty it silently processes every file it can find.
       The interface also now emits its metadata-read request after an EddyUH
       import, as it already did after an EddyPro one, so the Basic Settings
       page agrees with the records the conversion wrote.
	- New feature: The importer carries EddyUH's despiking across. spi_method 1
       is its consecutive-difference test, which is what despike_vm 1 does
       here, and dlim carries the per-column step limits - one per USED column
       in Columnorder's order, not one per raw column. A NaN limit means the
       column states none and is left undespiked. The wind and sonic
       temperature limits go on the project; a gas limit goes on its record,
       in that gas's own concentration unit.
       MaxNoSpikes is deliberately NOT carried across: EddyUH's is how many
       spikes a period may contain, EddyFlow's sr_num_spk is how many
       consecutive outliers make one spike. Similar names, different
       quantities. Reported instead.
       The importer also now says that EddyUH states no absolute limits for
       any gas - its dlim values are a different test - so the absolute-limits
       screening will not run unless the user sets them.
	- Note: The imported project was run end to end against a month of CH-LAE
       raw data and compared with the EddyUH flux file for the same month.
       On the 168 half-hourly periods that do not contain the site's hourly
       calibration, agreement is close: CO2 R2 0.983 on the MGA and 0.981 on
       the LI-7200, water 0.994 and 0.970, nitrous oxide 0.854, carbonyl
       sulfide 0.852, sensible heat 0.877, the w-Ts covariance 0.874, wind
       speed 1.000 - slopes between 0.91 and 1.18 throughout.
       On the 168 periods that DO contain the calibration, agreement
       collapses to R2 0.04-0.07. The valve opens for about two minutes every
       hour and carbonyl sulfide reaches 2e10 nmol/mol; EddyUH screened those
       periods out, and how it did so is not in its project files. This is the
       clearest illustration of what the import report warns about.
       Two departures from the EddyUH project were needed for the comparison
       and are NOT importer faults: the raw files here are 20 Hz where the
       project says 10 - EddyUH read a decimated copy that is not on this
       machine - and absolute limits had to be set by hand, EddyUH having
       none. Told 10 Hz against 20 Hz files, EddyFlow uses only the first half
       of every averaging period.
	- New feature: File > Import EddyUH Project. Reads an EddyUH project - the
       preproc_ file, with its lag, planar fit and response time siblings
       found beside it by stem - and fills an EddyFlow project and metadata
       pair. Nothing is written until you save, as with the EddyPro import:
       opening a project to look at it is not consent to write two files.
       What comes across: the site, the measurement height, the boom
       direction, the averaging interval and file length, the sampling rate,
       the raw file path and name template, the delimiter, the header rows and
       the timestamp convention, the rotation and detrending choices, the
       instruments with their tube geometry and separations, every raw column
       with its variable, unit, measure type and conversion, and the per-gas
       lag windows.
       What cannot come across is listed in the dialog after every import, and
       the list is never empty. EddyUH collects its flux-time options afresh
       at every run and writes them only to the text log beside the fluxes, so
       the spectral correction method, the cospectral model, the peak-frequency
       parameterisation, the time-lag method, the data screening and the
       footprint model are simply not in the project files. Nor is the
       longitude, nor the anemometer's north alignment.
       Some things are assumed rather than read, and each says so: an EddyUH
       inlet separation is one unsigned scalar where EddyFlow wants a signed
       northward and eastward pair, so it goes on the northward axis; a name
       like "Aerodyne cw-QCL" gives a manufacturer but not a model.
       One field is deliberately NOT imported. EddyUH stores a path length per
       gas analyser and uses it in no calculation - only the sonic's reaches
       EddyUH_TF.m - so its value is unchecked, and the supplied project holds
       76 for one analyser and 0.125 for the other, an optical path against a
       physical one. EddyFlow's spectral correction does use it, so importing
       it would turn a number that meant nothing into one that changes the
       answer.
       A raw column no instrument claims becomes Ignore rather than being
       dropped, which would shift every column after it by one. An
       unrecognised instrument or variable name is reported and left unset
       rather than guessed at.
       A one-dimensional rotation switches rotations OFF rather than being
       promoted to a double rotation, which would additionally null the
       vertical wind - the thing the project chose not to do.
       Verified against the supplied CH-LAE project: 398 of 483 metadata keys
       identical to the hand-built pair for the same site. Every difference is
       accounted for - the two projects name the trace-gas analyser
       differently and list the two analysers in opposite order, the hand-built
       one describes a raw file with four header rows against EddyUH's one,
       and the hand-built one predates the spectroscopic keys. The lag windows
       come across exactly: 16 +/- 1 s and 17 +/- 5 s on the QCL, 0.6 +/- 0.5 s
       and 4 +/- 4 s on the LI-7200.
       Pinned by tests/test_eddyuh_import_static.py.
	- Fix: MATLAB's ...StringList() accessors in this application are sorted
       alphabetically before they are returned, so the position of a string in
       one is not the number of the accessor that produced it. The first
       version of this importer indexed them, which turned raw column 1 into
       AGC and a Gill HS into an 81000RE - silently, in a file that looked
       plausible. Every lookup now calls its accessor directly.
	- Note: DlProject::toIniVariableInstrument splits a column's instrument
       label on a colon and then on a space and takes element 1 of each
       without checking, so a label not of the form "Sonic 1: HS-50" does not
       produce a wrong file - it crashes the save. The importer produces that
       form, and "Other" where it cannot. The underlying fragility is not
       fixed here.
	- New feature: "Metek USA-1 head correction" and "Inclinometer tilt correction"
       on the Advanced Processing Options page, between the angle-of-attack
       correction and the axis rotations - which is where they run. Write the
       engine's new head_corr_meth, head_corr_dir, tilt_sensor_meth,
       tilt_sensor_v_g, tilt_arm_x/y/z and tilt_lpf_s.
       Both keys are three-valued with off folded in, so each checkbox writes
       its combo's index plus one and zero when unticked. The loader refuses
       anything outside 0..2 by falling back to OFF rather than to one of the
       two corrections: guessing wrong here rewrites the wind itself.
       The lever arm greys with the MODE, not with the checkbox, because plain
       position correction never reads it and a live box invites a number that
       will be ignored.
       The labels cannot explain why a planar fit is not already enough, so the
       tooltips do: a rotation removes the mean tilt over a period, and this
       removes a tilt that changes within one. They also say where the angle
       columns come from - theta, phi and psi, declared in the Raw File
       Description like any other channel, there being no control for it here -
       that the columns hold volts rather than angles, and that psi is read and
       then thrown away.
       The Metek tooltip says plainly that the tables are Metek GmbH's data,
       are not shipped, and that without all three files the correction
       declines - otherwise a user ticks the box, walks away, and gets an
       unchanged answer with no indication why.
       The swinging mode's tooltip says that EddyUH adds a scalar where the
       physics wants a vector, and that adjusting the lever arm will not fix
       it. A user comparing against EddyUH wants that mode; a user who is not
       wants to avoid it; neither can tell from a label reading "Position and
       swinging".
       The page's grid grew six rows, so everything below the angle-of-attack
       correction moved down six.
       Pinned by tests/test_sonic_hardware_contract_static.py.
	- New feature: "Iterate the correction" on the Spectral Corrections page, under the
	       cospectral model, with a passes spin and a tolerance spin greyed with it.
	       Writes the engine's new corr_iter_meth, corr_iter_max and corr_iter_tol.
	       The checkbox label cannot explain why anything needs iterating, so the
	       tooltip does: the cospectrum is evaluated at z/L, z/L comes from the
	       corrected heat flux, and that flux is what the correction produces. It
	       also says that nothing compounds between passes - the obvious worry
	       about repeating a correction - where the effect is largest, and that the
	       corr_iter_dev column reports the outcome per period.
	       A tolerance of zero shows as "run every pass" rather than a bare 0,
	       because in a box labelled "stop below" a zero reads as "stop at once".
	       Zero is the default and it is what EddyUH does; the tolerance tooltip
	       says plainly that setting one departs from EddyUH and changes the
	       answer, since stopping at pass two is not stopping at pass four.
	       The loader keeps a zero tolerance and refuses only a negative one, and
	       refuses a pass count below one - which is what "off" already means.
	       Pinned by tests/test_corr_iteration_contract_static.py.
	- Fix: Two combos under the lag-borrowing checkbox: "Judged against" chooses
	       the noise floor, "Borrow from" chooses the donor. Both default to this
	       program's own choice, and the second entry on each says "(EddyUH)",
	       because being EddyUH's is the reason anyone would pick it.
	       It corrects a fidelity error: EddyUH judges a covariance against the
	       Lenschow instrument noise and borrows from the analyser's carbon
	       dioxide, where the original port used the Wienhold detection limit and
	       the best-resolved tube-mate.
	       The detection-limit dependency is conditional now. Only that floor is
	       computed elsewhere, so only that floor needs the setting on the
	       Statistical Analysis page switched on; the Lenschow floor is measured
	       from the series in hand. The noise combo itself stays enabled whenever
	       borrowing is ticked, even when the current choice is the unavailable
	       one - greying it along with everything else would strand a user whose
	       detection limit is off, because the control that fixes it is the one
	       they could not reach.
	       The carbon-dioxide tooltip states the two consequences the label
	       cannot: carbon dioxide can then never borrow, since it would be
	       borrowing from itself, and an analyser that measures none borrows
	       nothing at all.
	       An out-of-range value from a later version falls back to our own choice
	       rather than silently switching to EddyUH's. Both settings join the
	       comparison that marks a computed dataset stale.
	       The Processing Options grid grew two rows, so everything below the
	       borrowing block moved down two - including the quality-control block,
	       which would otherwise have collided with the rule above it.
	       Pinned by tests/test_tlag_borrow_contract_static.py.
	- New feature: Lenschow et al. (2000) joins the random uncertainty menu as a fourth
	       entry, writing the engine's ru_meth = 5. It estimates the analyser's own
	       white noise from the step in the autocovariance at zero lag.
	       Its tooltip carries the three things the menu itself cannot show. That
	       this is the INSTRUMENT's noise, not a sampling error like the two above
	       it and not a resolvability floor like Billesbach - four entries all
	       producing a number in the same units, answering three different
	       questions. That it DECLINES a period when its assumption fails, so a
	       column of missing values is the method working rather than the run
	       failing. And that its five-lag window is fixed in samples, so it spans
	       half as long at 20 Hz as at 10.
	       The stored value is 5 on row 3: the row and the method number have not
	       run in step since 3 became Mahrt, and nothing here derives one from the
	       other.
	       Pinned by tests/test_lenschow_noise_contract_static.py.
	- New feature: A third despiking method on the Statistical Analysis page,
	       "Consecutive difference (EddyUH)", writing despike_vm = 2. It replaces a
	       sample that steps further from the one before it than a stated limit, and
	       counts it as a spike.
	       Its settings sit in a column of their own rather than beside the existing
	       ones, because they are a different kind of number: the two methods above
	       take sigma multipliers and this one takes ABSOLUTE limits in each
	       variable's own unit. Side by side in the same column would invite reading
	       one as the other. Four sonic spins - u, v, w and sonic temperature, since
	       a kelvin is not a metre per second - and a step column in the per-gas
	       table beside the plausibility one.
	       The radio's id IS the ini value: 0, 1, 2, with 2 for the new arm.
	       Renumbering the group to keep its rows contiguous would silently change
	       the method of every project that states one, so the third button carries
	       id 2 and the refresh falls back to Vickers for anything it does not know.
	       A limit of zero shows as "not despiked" rather than a bare 0, on every one
	       of those spins, because that is what it means and because the engine will
	       then pass the column over and say so in the run log. The per-gas default
	       is zero deliberately: an absolute step cannot be guessed from the species
	       when the unit belongs to the column. The four sonic defaults are EddyUH's
	       own values for the CH-LAE project - 10, 10, 5 and 10.
	       The method and all four limits join the comparison that marks a computed
	       dataset stale, since despiking changes the series everything later is
	       computed from.
	       Pinned by tests/test_despike_step_contract_static.py.
	- New feature: A "Cospectral model" choice under the low-pass correction methods on
	       the Spectral Corrections page, writing the engine's new cosp_model.
	       Six curves: Moncrieff et al. (1997) - the default, and what this
	       program has always used - plus Kaimal et al. (1972), Sakai et al.
	       (2001), Su et al. (2003), Moraes et al. (2008) and Kristensen et al.
	       (1997). Each row carries its own citation tooltip.
	       It sits directly under a list of correction METHODS, which is exactly
	       why the tooltip opens by saying it is a modifier on whichever of them
	       is selected and not a seventh entry: it changes the shape they are all
	       integrated against, and on the test fixture that moves the gas
	       correction factors by about four percent between the extremes.
	       The tooltips carry the three things a reader could not get from the
	       names. That the Reynolds stress keeps Moncrieff's momentum cospectrum
	       whichever is chosen, because four of the six are scalar cospectra with
	       no momentum form. That those four have no stability branch either, so
	       under stable stratification they understate the high-frequency loss.
	       And that the library is EddyUH's diagnostic curves rather than
	       EddyUH's method - EddyUH corrects against measured and fitted
	       cospectra - so picking one is not reproducing EddyUH.
	       The combo row IS the ini value, with no mapping in between, so the
	       engine's decode table is not duplicated here. An out-of-range value
	       from a later version falls back to the default on load rather than
	       leaving the combo showing Moncrieff while the file says otherwise.
	       Pinned by tests/test_cosp_model_contract_static.py.
	- New feature: "Borrow a tube-mate's lag below the detection limit" and its "Detection
	       limits to clear" threshold, beside the baseline checkbox, writing the
	       engine's new tlag_borrow_meth and tlag_borrow_snr. A species whose
	       cross-covariance peak cannot be told from noise takes the lag of the
	       best-resolved gas on the same analyser rather than a nominal one
	       (Nemitz et al., 2018). Default 3.0 detection limits, the paper's value.
	       Both controls are greyed until the flux detection limit is on, because
	       the test has nothing to compare against without it - and since that
	       setting lives on the <i>Statistical Analysis</i> page, the tooltip names
	       where to switch it on rather than leaving the user to hunt for what
	       disabled a control on this one. The engine refuses the same combination
	       independently, so a hand-written project cannot get past it either.
	       The tooltip also states the two things a reader would otherwise have to
	       find out from a run: that water is never borrowed for or from, and that
	       a borrowed lag is flagged in <i>&lt;gas&gt;_def_timelag</i> with the
	       donor named in the run log. Off by default. Pinned by
	       tests/test_tlag_borrow_contract_static.py.
	- New feature: "Subtract the cross-covariance baseline", under the time-lag method
	       it modifies, writing the engine's new covmax_debaseline. It chooses the lag
	       by the largest departure from the straight line joining the two ends of the
	       search window rather than by the largest absolute covariance, which is what
	       a weak flux on a sloping cross-covariance needs. Greyed unless the method
	       is one of the two that maximise a covariance - Constant does not search,
	       and the optimiser and the block-bootstrap choose their lags by other
	       machinery.
	       The tooltip says explicitly that it changes <b>which lag is selected</b>
	       and nothing about the covariance reported there, because a checkbox in the
	       corrections half of that page reads like a correction otherwise. It also
	       warns that with the baseline removed the window ends score zero by
	       construction, so the maximum can never land on one - which means
	       <i>Covariance maximization with default</i> stops falling back to the
	       nominal lag. Off by default.
	- Fix: The spectroscopic coefficient tooltips say that the values are spectroscopic
	       only, and how to convert a published one. EddyUH divides by the same
	       polynomial but folds the dilution into it - its a = -1 is pure dilution -
	       while EddyFlow corrects the density separately and its identity is zero.
	       Someone holding a Rella (2010) number and a box to type it into had nothing
	       telling them the two are not the same quantity, and entering it unchanged
	       would count the dilution twice. Both the Processing Options checkbox and the
	       Spectroscopic a column header now say so, and give the rule: add one to the
	       EddyUH value. Pinned by tests/test_spectroscopic_contract_static.py.
	- New feature: "Remove the spectroscopic effect of water vapour", on the Processing
	       Options page beside the density compensation, writing the engine's new
	       spectro_meth. Water broadens the absorption lines a laser analyser
	       measures, so each affected column is divided sample by sample by
	       1 + a*chi_q + b*chi_q^2, using the water its own analyser read at the same
	       instant. Deliberately not gated on WPL, and the tooltip says why: WPL is a
	       density correction and this is an optical one, and the bias is in what the
	       instrument reported whether or not densities are being compensated.
	       The per-column a and b are two new rows in the Raw File Description, named
	       Spectroscopic a and b so that nobody confuses them with Gain value and
	       Offset value directly above, which are the linear calibration and have
	       nothing to do with this - both tooltips say so. Zero, the default, leaves
	       the column untouched.
	- New feature: "Also correct the water channel (EddyUH form, unpublished)", beneath
	       it and greyed until it is switched on. It applies the same division to each
	       hygrometer against its own reading, which is self-broadening. The tooltip
	       states plainly that this is <b>not part of the published Peltola et al.
	       (2014) result</b>, that EddyUH does it with a coefficient whose derivation
	       its own source says is not published anywhere, and that it should be
	       selected deliberately and reported. Off by default.
	- New feature: Billesbach (2011) joins the random uncertainty menu, writing the
	       engine's ru_meth = 4. It estimates a noise floor by reordering each scalar
	       at random twenty times and averaging the covariance that survives: what
	       is left after the shuffle was produced by noise alone. The tooltip says
	       plainly that this is a noise floor and not a sampling error - it answers
	       whether a flux is resolvable rather than how uncertain it is, and is
	       systematically smaller than the other two - because the three sit in one
	       menu and nothing else on the page would tell you they answer different
	       questions.
	- Fix: The random uncertainty menu maps to ru_meth by stored value rather than by
	       row number. It was read as currentIndex() + 1, which held only while the
	       menu listed exactly the first two methods: the engine's 3 is Mahrt (1998),
	       which the menu does not offer because the engine computes it
	       unconditionally, so the third entry is Billesbach at 4 and its position
	       says nothing about its value. Left as arithmetic, adding the entry would
	       have selected Mahrt while displaying Billesbach - no error, just a
	       different method running under the wrong name. Four sites did it; all four
	       now go through item data, and a project holding a value the menu does not
	       list leaves the row alone instead of blanking it.
	- New feature: A "Flux Detection Limit..." dialog, on the Statistical Analysis
	       page beneath the random uncertainty group, writing the engine's new
	       detlim_meth, detlim_offset_s and detlim_window_s. It estimates the noise
	       floor of each gas's covariance with vertical wind after Wienhold et al.
	       (1994), by measuring the scatter of the cross-covariance function away
	       from its peak: a flux smaller than its detection limit cannot be told
	       from zero. Worth having for weak-flux species - on the test dataset
	       carbonyl sulfide clears three times its limit in 10 half-hours out of 48.
	       Off by default, and switching it on changes no flux: it only reports the
	       limit alongside. The two window settings offer Wienhold's own values, 100
	       s and 50 s, so that enabling the feature reproduces the published method
	       rather than something chosen here; tests/test_detlim_engine_contract_static.py
	       compares them against the engine's literals so the two cannot drift.
	       A dialog rather than controls on the page itself: that grid reserves its
	       first column for the random-error checkbox and pins a stretch below the
	       last live row, so a method plus two dependent windows does not sit in it
	       without a rebuild.
	       It is deliberately independent of the random uncertainty checkbox above
	       it - unlike the CEC significance test, which needs a random error to
	       compare against. These two answer different questions, how uncertain is
	       this flux against can this flux be told from zero, and either is useful
	       without the other.
	       The dialog warns when the window is as wide as its own offset or wider,
	       which is the one way to configure this so that it measures the flux
	       instead of the noise under it. The engine refuses that combination and
	       falls back to the defaults; the warning is the same rule, shown while the
	       numbers are being set rather than afterwards in the log.
	- New feature: A checkbox for the engine's new test_rf (Advanced Settings ->
	               Statistical Analysis, "Extra raw-signal diagnostics (RFlux)"),
	               off by default. Adapted from RFlux's inst_prob_test() (Vitale
	               et al. 2020, Biogeosciences) - lag-1 autocorrelation, the
	               discrete/dominant-value test, robust-scaled spike counts, and
	               (added after this checkbox first landed) Hartigan's dip test
	               p-value for multimodality (Hartigan & Hartigan 1985), cited
	               separately from the RFlux tests in its own tooltip line since
	               it has its own, older, more precise reference.
	               Deliberately outside checkbox_list/areAllCheckedTests/
	               selectAllTest and the toolbox: it has no parameter page of its
	               own, and "select all" restoring project defaults should not
	               silently turn on a diagnostic that widens the output file.
	               Wired through all four places a screen-test flag needs:
	               saveEcProject, loadEcProject, the fuzzyCompare chain and
	               newEcProject - the last being where PWB's own flag was missed
	               entirely when it was added.
	- New feature: A checkbox for the engine's new test_pfd (Advanced Settings ->
	               Statistical Analysis, "Post-flux despiking (STL)"), off by
	               default. Adapted from RFlux's despiking(variant="v1"): unlike
	               the raw-signal tests above it, this one runs once per run,
	               after every flux is computed, on the finished half-hourly
	               NEE/H/LE series rather than on raw high-frequency data - so it
	               is wired through generalTestPfd()/setGeneralTestPfd() in the
	               [Project] general settings, not the per-test screenTest
	               family the raw-data checkboxes above use.
	               Deliberately outside checkbox_list/areAllCheckedTests/
	               selectAllTest, for the same reason test_rf is: it has no
	               parameter page of its own, and it needs at least 20 periods
	               and 4 periods/day to run at all, so "select all" silently
	               turning it on would be misleading on a short project.
	               Wired through the same four places as every other project
	               setting: ecproject.h's getter/setter, saveEcProject,
	               loadEcProject and newEcProject. Not part of fuzzyCompare, for
	               the same reason corr_iter_meth is not - it runs as a
	               standalone post-pass over already-written output, so it
	               cannot make a computed dataset stale.
	- New feature: A fourth entry in the Quality check combo (Advanced
	               Settings -> Processing Options), "Vitale et al. (2020)
	               (0-1-2 severity system)", for the engine's new qc_meth
	               option. A plain fourth qcMethodCombo item, the same shape
	               as the three it joins - no new checkbox, no new ecproject
	               field, since qcflag_meth/generalQcMeth() already carry any
	               integer the engine's select case accepts. The tooltip
	               names what feeds it - the ITC deviation always, and, on
	               a project that does not hand spectral corrections off
	               to FCC, Mahrt's 1998 nonstationarity ratio, the always-
	               on KID test and (when Extra raw-signal diagnostics is
	               also on) AL1/DDI/HF/HD/DIP too - and says plainly both
	               what RFlux's cleanFlux() this leaves out
	               (low-signal-resolution, physical-range) and that the
	               wider set drops to ITC-only on the more common project
	               that does hand off to FCC, rather than implying either
	               parity with cleanFlux() or that the wider set always
	               applies. That second caveat was missing from the
	               tooltip's first wording; corrected once discovered.

8.0.0 (2026-08-20)

    GUI:
	- New feature: "Only partition a resolvable flux", in CEC Settings, writing the
	       engine's new cec_min_flux_sigma. Refuses a pairing whose water or carbon
	       flux is smaller than the given number of times its own random error,
	       instead of splitting a signal that is not there: the octants mean
	       something only if the sign of c' carries a surface signature, and where
	       the scalar is barely coupled to the vertical wind that sign is noise and
	       the moist ejections split near evenly between O1 and O2. Ticking it
	       switches on random uncertainty estimation - as ticking CEC switches on
	       WPL, and for the same kind of reason: the test has nothing to compare a
	       flux against until the random error is being estimated - and it selects
	       Finkelstein and Sims (2001), the estimator whose value makes the
	       comparison a statement about the correlation rather than about an assumed
	       eddy size. That stays the user's to switch off, and a warning triangle is
	       lit while the project is in the state where the test cannot run. The
	       tooltip says which way is stricter and that the paper suggests no value at
	       all. Unticked - the default - reproduces the published method, and Restore
	       Default Values puts it back. The box offers 2 when switched on.
	- New feature: "Judge the partition, not the flux", beside the stationarity limit in
	       CEC Settings. Unchecked - the default - is Zahn et al. (2022), which
	       drops a period when Foken's non-stationarity statistic for w'c' or
	       w'q' exceeds the limit. That statistic divides by the covariance it is
	       testing, so it runs away whenever the flux is near zero, which at
	       night it is, on periods whose octants are perfectly well sampled.
	       Checked, the criterion becomes whether the split between the two
	       octants holds steady through the period - the same six sub-intervals,
	       each re-centred on its own mean, but normalised by the size of the
	       partition rather than by the flux, so it cannot run away. The
	       statistic is written out either way as cec_ns_<species>, bounded by
	       200, so the two criteria can be compared on one run. Leave it
	       unchecked to reproduce the published method; Restore Default Values
	       puts it back.
	- Fix: A measurement record whose column has since been re-declared is dropped instead
	       of being left behind. Pick a column as an analyser's diagnostic, then
	       change that column in the Raw File Description to something else, and the
	       record naming it survived the edit. The column left the Variables table
	       with it, so the row that would show the record was gone and the record
	       could not be un-ticked: invisible here, and fatal in the engine, which
	       found two records claiming its one diagnostic slot and refused to
	       process the project. Cell and diagnostic records are checked against the
	       Raw File Description on every metadata read now, and one that
	       contradicts it is removed. Gas records are deliberately untouched: they
	       carry per-species processing settings that dropping one would discard.
	- Fix: An AGC or RSSI column is no longer offered as a gas. It reached the custom-species
	       branch of the Variables table, so a signal-strength percentage could be
	       selected as something to compute a flux of.
	- Change: A declared AGC or RSSI column is written into the project file as a record of
	       its own - agc_<i>_var, agc_<i>_instr, agc_<i>_col - beside the gas, cell
	       and diagnostic records. The engine had nothing but the metadata variable
	       name to go on, matched case-sensitively, with the analyser inferred; the
	       record says which column and which analyser outright, so the conditional
	       eddy covariance screen can be aimed at the right one. The records are
	       derived from the Raw File Description rather than selected, and rebuilt
	       on every metadata read, so there is nothing to tick and nothing that can
	       go stale.
	- New feature: AGC and RSSI can be chosen in the Raw File Description. Neither was
	       in the variable list, so a signal-strength column could not be declared
	       from here at all - and the engine's conditional eddy covariance screen
	       looks for exactly those two names on the gas's own analyser, so on most
	       projects it silently had nothing to screen by. They are written verbatim
	       and are not translated, because the engine's comparison is
	       case-sensitive; a localised name would be stored and then never matched.
	       They take the same cell treatment as the diagnostic words: dimensionless,
	       no measure type, no conversion.
	- Change: The signal-strength cutoff in CEC Settings says when it cannot do anything.
	       It is greyed, with a warning triangle, when no analyser in the Raw File
	       Description declares an AGC or RSSI column - previously it sat there
	       offering a threshold that was never applied. Where only some analysers
	       declare one it stays enabled, because the screen genuinely runs for
	       those, and the triangle names the ones whose gases go unscreened. Both
	       tooltips say that the partition itself still runs and only this screening
	       step is skipped, so a greyed box is not read as CEC being broken.
	- Fix: variable_desc builds without warnings. Four members were declared in a
	       different order from the one all three constructors initialise them in,
	       and the compiler said so on every build of the file. Harmless as it stood
	       - they are plain reals that do not read each other - but a real -Wreorder
	       is how a member gets initialised from one that is still garbage.
	- Change: Switching Conditional Eddy Covariance on also switches on the compensation
	       of density fluctuations. The partition sorts each air parcel into an
	       octant by the SIGN of its water and carbon dioxide fluctuation, and the
	       engine converts the raw series to mixing ratios only when WPL is on; in an
	       uncorrected molar density from an open-path analyser, part of that
	       fluctuation is the air expanding rather than the gas arriving, and it is
	       large enough to reverse the sign and put the parcel in the wrong octant.
	       The correction can still be switched back off - a closed-path analyser
	       reporting mixing ratios is unaffected - but doing so while the partition
	       is on now says why that is a bad idea, and leaves a warning triangle
	       beside the setting for as long as the project stays in that state. A
	       project saved that way opens unchanged and without a dialog, but with the
	       triangle already showing. The run reports the same thing as Warning(114).
	- Fix: The Conditional Eddy Covariance tooltip comes back after the checkbox has
	       been unavailable. The restore read the tooltip it was about to overwrite,
	       so once a site without both channels had put the "unavailable" text there,
	       that was what got restored and the real tooltip was gone for the rest of
	       the session.
	- Fix: The minus signs in the singularity-band tooltip are the characters they were
	       meant to be. Written as a UTF-8 escape immediately before a digit -
	       \xe2\x88\x92 then 1 - the escape swallowed the digit, because a C++ hex
	       escape takes as many hex digits as follow rather than two. Every "-1",
	       "-1.2" and "-0.8" in that tooltip was corrupted, and the compiler had been
	       saying so. A check now refuses the pattern anywhere in the sources.
	- Change: The small-gap-fill tooltip says what the setting now does: it repairs runs
	       of condemned samples AFTER the signal-strength screen, which is the order
	       Zahn et al. (2022) state and which the engine has been corrected to follow,
	       and 0 turns it off for anyone who would rather lose the period than
	       interpolate across an analyser that was not seeing.
	- Fix: Opening an EddyPro project no longer writes anything. It used to write two files
	       before the user had seen the project: <base>.eddyflow deliberately, and then the
	       user's own .metadata by accident. The second is the one that mattered - reopening
	       drove DlProject::loadProject over the EddyPro metadata, any retired Campbell model
	       key set legacyInstrumentSlugs, that called setModified(true), and projectModified is
	       wired to saveAvailable, which writes. So a Campbell site's metadata was rewritten in
	       place, with no backup, by the act of looking at it.
	       The conversion happens in memory now and stops there. What comes out is an ordinary
	       unsaved document - modified, never saved - which is the same state File > New
	       produces and which the rest of the interface already understands: Save routes to
	       Save As, closing prompts, and the Run buttons refuse until it is on disk. Save opens
	       the Save As dialog pre-filled with <base>.eddyflow beside the .eddypro.
	- New feature: The metadata is written when the project is, not before, and to a name that
	               cannot be the file it was read from. It goes beside the project under the
	               project's own base name, with proj_file pointed at it; on a straight import
	               that name IS the EddyPro metadata, so that case takes _ep_imported instead -
	               the same suffix, for the same reason, as the engine's own importer. Paths are
	               compared canonically, because the two spellings arrive from different places
	- Fix: An EddyPro project opens from the command line, from Recent Files, and by
	       double-clicking on macOS. The redirect to the importer sat inside the branch that
	       only runs when the user picked the file from the dialog, so every other route fell
	       through to the native-format check and was told the file was "not in EddyFlow native
	       format" - which of an EddyPro project is true and useless. The macOS Finder filter
	       accepted .eddypro and dropped .eddyflow, so the fork's own projects could not be
	       double-clicked at all; it takes both now
	- Fix: An imported project whose proj_file names a metadata that is not on this machine
	       falls back to the file of the project's own name beside it. proj_file is an absolute
	       path from wherever the project was made, so a project that has been copied anywhere
	       names a path that is not there - and without the fallback the import has no metadata
	       to resolve its gas records against, which leaves every species blank. This is the
	       rule the engine's importer already follows
	- Fix: The silent metadata cleanup leaves an unsaved project alone. It clears proj_file and
	       calls fileSave, which for a project that has never been saved is Save As - a save
	       dialog in front of somebody who has only just opened something
	- BREAKING: The project format goes to 5.1.0, with the rebuilt Conditional Eddy Covariance
	          settings. An older engine refuses such a file rather than ignoring the parts of it
	          it does not know.
	- New feature: A "Channel pairing" table in CEC Settings says which CO2 channel is
	          partitioned against which H2O channel. It defaults to one pairing per CO2 channel,
	          each with the water on the same analyser, which is what a site means when it has
	          not said - and is what the engine derives on its own from an unstated project. A
	          pairing that crosses two analysers is marked, because the octants are built from
	          the water and the carbon together and across two instruments those two arrive at
	          different time lags and through different spectral responses.
	          Which of the pairing's two fluxes are reported moved into the same table, one
	          setting per pairing.
	- New feature: Each pairing can carry further species to partition in the same octants,
	          chosen from the gases the site measures. Carbonyl sulfide is the useful case: its
	          plant uptake is stomatal and effectively irreversible, so the stomatal component is
	          a cleaner photosynthesis proxy than the CO2 one.
	- New feature: A "Singularity band" setting, defaulting to the 0.2 Zahn et al. (2022) use.
	          The engine had it hard-coded at a quarter of that.
	- Fix: Conditional Eddy Covariance requires BOTH a CO2 and an H2O channel, and says so
	          when one is missing. It used to accept either and force the choice to whichever was
	          present - and the engine, which needs both to decide which octant a parcel is in,
	          then refused to run, so the project got a full set of columns containing nothing
	          but the error code with nothing anywhere explaining why.
	- Change: The global three-way "Flux partitioning" combo is retired. It said nothing useful
	          once a site could have a pairing per analyser, and it was what forced the
	          impossible choice above. The checkbox is now a master switch.
	- Fix: The three occupancy limits are percentages and the engine now reads them as such
	          unconditionally. It used to pick between a percentage and a fraction by magnitude,
	          so a box labelled [%] and set to 0.5 came back as fifty per cent.
	- Fix: The hyperbolic threshold's tooltip describes what the engine does. It cited MREA's
	          dimensionless H = 0.25 while the engine compared against the raw product, where no
	          value of H means anything in particular; the engine is dimensionless now and the
	          text says so, including that raising H also thins the octants.
	- Fix: The Conditional Eddy Covariance checkbox has the documentation link every other
	          checkbox in its block has, and its tooltip names the columns the engine actually
	          writes - Reco_cec, not R_cec.
	- Change: The "Detect time lag on raw data" control is retired, and with it the choice it
	          offered. It never reached the engine in the first place: this wrote the setting as
	          pwb_detect_on_raw while the engine declared pwb_detect_prewpl, and the engine matches
	          tag labels by exact equality, so the box was inert for every project ever saved
	          whatever it showed. Renaming it would have switched on, for everybody at once, a pass
	          that had never run.
	          It is gone instead, because there is no longer anything to choose. Its tooltip
	          described a decision between raw and block-averaged data and there was never such a
	          decision: both settings ran on rotated 20 Hz data, and the flag only ever selected
	          whether the gas series had already been through the mixing-ratio conversion. That
	          conversion runs before time-lag compensation, so detecting after it puts cell
	          temperature and water into the gas series at the wrong relative lag - and the gas
	          series is the one being cross-correlated. Detection is pre-WPL now, always.
	          Both spellings are removed from any project this saves, so no dead key is left behind
	          for somebody to edit in the belief that it matters
	- Fix: The PWB smoothing width defaults to 5, as the engine does. It defaulted to 6 here,
	       and because this writes the key unconditionally, every project it saved carried a 6
	       while a project without the key got the engine's 5 - two defaults for one setting,
	       with nothing anywhere to say they disagreed. A project already saved keeps whatever
	       it states; only a new one changes
	- New feature: A check that reads BOTH repositories: every PWB key this writes must appear
	               in the engine's tag table, and the defaults the two share must agree.
	               Nothing at build time connects them, so a name or a default can drift with
	               both sides compiling and running perfectly - which is how both fixes above
	               went unnoticed. It skips when the engine is not checked out beside this tree,
	               like the other cross-repo checks here
	- New feature: Every anemometer and gas analyzer carries an acquisition frequency, in the
	               Metadata File Editor's instrument tables. It shows the station's frequency
	               until the instrument is given one of its own - and goes on following it, so
	               changing the station's rate changes every instrument that has not been set
	               apart from it. Stating a lower rate is what stops the gaps a slower
	               instrument leaves in the raw file being counted as missing data; before
	               this, such a column was dropped and its flux was -9999
	- New feature: Every raw column carries an error value, in the Raw File Description table -
	               what that column writes when the instrument had no reading, in the column's own
	               unit. -9999 by default, which the engine treats as missing whether or not a
	               column declares it, along with NaN, an unparseable word such as NA, and an
	               empty field; the field only has to be changed when the logger writes something
	               else. Recognised in the raw file before any unit conversion, because a fill
	               value is only recognisable while it still looks like itself
	- New feature: Absolute limits are seeded per species rather than from one shared "other"
	               pair. N2O and COS used to get the same window though they differ by three
	               orders of magnitude. CO2, CH4, N2O and H2O keep the ceilings in force today;
	               COS takes 0.01 and CO 5 umol/mol, well above their measured backgrounds -
	               CO's is loose on purpose, since urban air reaches several umol/mol and a
	               limit deletes the sample rather than flagging it;
	               N2, O2 and Ar state their own because a generic ceiling would sit orders below
	               their abundance; anything else takes 5000 of the unit trace gases are quoted
	               in, so 5 umol/mol. All in the stored basis - umol/mol, mmol/mol for water -
	               and pinned by a check that no ceiling may fall below what the atmosphere
	               actually contains, since a limit deletes the sample rather than flagging it
	- New feature: Every instrument carries a sampling mode beside its acquisition frequency:
	               "Instantaneous" or "Averaged over the interval". It decides how the vertical
	               wind is paired with that instrument's samples when its cospectrum is built,
	               and only matters for an instrument slower than the station - so it is greyed
	               out until one is given a rate of its own. Instantaneous is the default
	- New feature: Basic Settings carries a missing-samples allowance per instrument, beside
	               the project-wide one. Left at "Same as above", an instrument uses the
	               project-wide allowance and follows it when it changes; the key is written
	               only for the instruments that state one of their own. The rows are numbered
	               the way the metadata numbers instruments - anemometers first, then analysers
	               - and are rebuilt whenever the metadata is reloaded
	- Bugfix: The spectral assessment file test reads the file's own structure instead of counting
	          rows against the shipped sample. It assumed 14 rows per non-water gas from a fixed
	          row 19, so it was wrong for any project with a second hygrometer - which adds an
	          11-row humidity block - and it compared block headers whole, so every trailing
	          token the engine added (groups=, var=, instr=, exp=) made a good file look broken.
	          A block is now found by the word TFP in its header and sized by whether that header
	          says "numerosity", which is how the engine itself reads it. The warning also no
	          longer claims the engine will ignore the file
	- Bugfix: Refreshed the sample assessment file, which still carried a preamble line the engine
	          stopped writing and a block for the retired fourth gas slot
	- Bugfix: Species molecular weights carry six figures, matching the engine. CO2 and N2O were
	          both 44.01 and so appeared identical; they are 44.0095 and 44.0128. CH4 and H2O
	          moved with them, to 16.0425 and 18.0153
	- Bugfix: Gases preselected from the metadata file are given their species' processing settings.
	          They were created with none, so a project wrote no sr_lim, al_min/al_max, ds_hf/ds_sf,
	          tl_def or time-lag windows for exactly the site's main gases - and the engine reads an
	          absent threshold as "not configured" and declines the test. The pages showed plausible
	          defaults throughout, because a spin box falls back to the species default for an unset
	          field, so nothing on screen said the values were never saved
	- Bugfix: A project already saved without those settings is repaired when it is opened. The
	          legacy migration could not do it: it only ran on files predating the gas records
	- Bugfix: Time lag optimization file browse no longer opens a second, redundant file dialog
	- Bugfix: The instrument tables show every row, and each label sits on its own row. The row
	          labels are a widget of QLabels beside the table rather than the table's own header, and
	          nothing tied the two together: the container's height was a hard-coded number sized for the
	          row count of the day, and with the vertical scroll bar off the rows past it could not be
	          reached - the analyzer table lost Acquisition frequency and Sampling entirely. The labels
	          were also left to size themselves, so one taller than a row, such as the extinction
	          coefficients' subscripts, took its extra from the rest and pushed every label below it off
	          its row, which is how the acquisition frequency came to read against Time response. Each
	          view now states the height it needs from its own row count, every label is pinned to one
	          row, and the block starts where row 0 starts instead of half a row above it. The Raw File
	          Description table had the same two faults and is fixed with them
	- Fix: A project file written with Windows path separators can be opened without destroying its
	       paths. QSettings reads INI values through an unescaper, and drops any escape it does not
	       recognise together with the character after it - and every separator in a native Windows
	       path introduces one. Measured, not supposed:
	       "C:\Users\jonmuell\Documents\_data\CH-LAE COS\lae_biomet.csv" came back as
	       "C:sersonmuellocumentsdataH-LAE COSae_biomet.csv". Saving then wrote that back, so a
	       single open-and-save destroyed the path permanently and the engine was left opening a file
	       that could not exist.
	       Nothing can recover it afterwards - by the time QSettings hands the value over, the
	       characters are gone - so separators are now converted before QSettings sees the file, and
	       the project is marked modified so that saving keeps the repair. A file this interface
	       wrote is already forward-slashed and is passed through untouched; only a hand-edited or
	       third-party file takes the copy, and the user is told it happened
	- Fix: The PWB smoothing width accepts either parity. A centred rolling mean of even width has
	       no midpoint, and the engine used to widen it by a sample rather than follow R, so an
	       even value quietly meant something other than what it said. The engine follows zoo's
	       align="center" convention for both parities now - an odd window is symmetric, an even
	       one puts its extra sample after the centre - which makes the paper's own value reachable:
	       hz/2 + 1 is 6 at 10 Hz.
	       The tooltip says what the choice costs. On the reference implementation's data, widening
	       from 5 to 11 at 20 Hz widened the 95% interval from 0.00/0.05 s to 0.30/0.20 s, against
	       the 0.5 s threshold that decides whether a detection is accepted at all
	- New feature: A "Max carry" setting bounds how far a detected time lag may travel to an
	               averaging period that detected none - all three ways a gas reaches its own
	               lag, interpolation between reliable neighbours, carrying the last one forward
	               and filling backward from the next, because bounding one of them alone
	               achieves nothing. Past it the period takes the lag of another gas on the same
	               analyser instead, and failing that the gas's median.
	               In elapsed hours rather than in averaging periods, because the engine's table
	               has a row only where a period was processed: counting periods would reach
	               straight across a gap in the raw files. Twenty-four hours by default; zero is
	               the published rule, under which one reliable half hour can supply days
	- Change: The gap-filling note says what the engine now does, which is the reverse of what it
	          said. A gas's OWN lag comes first in all three of its forms, and a lag borrowed
	          from a neighbour on the same analyser only after them: two gases down one tube
	          still have measurably different delays, so borrowing trades a stale number for a
	          biased one. It also states the two rules a reader cannot infer from the controls -
	          that a lag is never taken from a different instrument, nor from water, and that a
	          gas whose record names no instrument neither donates nor borrows, since nothing
	          then proves it shares a tube with anything
	- Change: The block-length setting is described as what it is, a floor. The engine derives
	          the block per gas as max(this, 2 x that gas's widest search bound), so a gas
	          searching to 25 s resamples in 50 s blocks whatever is set here - a block shorter
	          than the lag range cannot contain the structure the bootstrap exists to preserve
	- Change: The "Speed options" group is gone. Both options cost accuracy and bought almost
	          nothing, and both tooltips overstated the gain by one to two orders of magnitude.
	          "Use approximate CCF" claimed 2-3x for skipping the CCF normalisation; that saves
	          two passes over the series against one per lag, well under a percent, and because
	          the divisor is constant across lags it changed nothing WITHIN a combination while
	          deciding the winner BETWEEN them by unit scale - the four pair the scalar against
	          vertical wind and against sonic temperature, whose covariances are not in the same
	          units. That is a systematic bias on every period, not the "shift of +/-1 sample in
	          rare edge cases" the tooltip described. "Cap AR model order" claimed 10-15% of
	          total PWB time; AR fitting is about a percent of it, and capping under-fits the
	          pre-whitener, which is the step that sharpens the peak the method exists to find.
	          The engine gained a guard band and hoisted buffers in their place, which cost
	          nothing scientifically and are worth appreciably more
	- Change: The "Detect time lag on raw data" checkbox is gone, replaced by a line saying what
	          actually happens. Its tooltip described a choice between raw and block-averaged
	          data; there was never such a choice. Both settings ran on rotated 20 Hz data and the
	          flag only ever chose whether the gas series had already been through the
	          mixing-ratio conversion. Detection is pre-WPL now, with nothing left to pick
	- Fix: The per-gas search windows show the window that will actually be searched. Both spins
	       displayed 0.0 when a gas had no window stored while the engine searched [-10, +10], so
	       the dialog reported a window nobody was using - and because setting a spin to the value
	       it already displays emits nothing, a user who wanted a minimum of 0 could not say so:
	       the record kept its sentinel and the engine went on searching from -10. The sentinel
	       moved off -1.0 too, which is an ordinary bound a user may want to state
	- Change: The HDI prefilter tooltip says what it does, now that it does something. Nothing in
	          the engine had ever read that setting

